## Proof

Number the roads (edges) by increasing toll price: $e_1, e_2, \dots, e_m$, with distinct weights $w(e_1) < w(e_2) < \cdots < w(e_m)$. Let $G_k$ denote the subgraph keeping only the edges $e_1, \dots, e_k$.

**Key characterization.** For every $k$,
$$e_k \text{ is closed} \iff e_k \text{ lies on some Hamiltonian path of } G_k.$$

*Proof of the characterization.* If $e_k$ is closed, then $e_k$ is the most expensive edge of some Hamiltonian path $P$. Every other edge of $P$ is cheaper than $e_k$, hence belongs to $\{e_1,\dots,e_{k-1}\}$; together with $e_k$ this means $P$ is a Hamiltonian path of $G_k$ using $e_k$. Conversely, if $P$ is a Hamiltonian path of $G_k$ using $e_k$, then every edge of $P$ has weight $\le w(e_k)$, and since $e_k$ is the heaviest edge of $G_k$ (weights are distinct), $e_k$ is strictly the most expensive edge of $P$. So $e_k$ is closed. $\square$

Now assume the king's order was followed correctly, and let $R$ be the set of closed edges.

**Existence of a bottleneck path.** If no Hamiltonian path exists at all, then $R = \varnothing$ by the characterization, so $G \setminus R = G$ is connected — contradicting the claim that three cities became pairwise disconnected. So a Hamiltonian path exists. Let

$$k^* = \min\{\, k : G_k \text{ has a Hamiltonian path}\,\},$$

and pick a Hamiltonian path $P^*$ of $G_{k^*}$.

**The heaviest edge of $P^*$ is exactly $e_{k^*}$.** Indeed, all edges of $P^*$ lie in $G_{k^*}$, so the heaviest edge of $P^*$ is some $e_j$ with $j \le k^*$. If $j < k^*$, then $P^*$ would be a Hamiltonian path of $G_j \subseteq G_{k^*-1}$, contradicting the minimality of $k^*$. Hence $j = k^*$, and $P^*$ uses $e_{k^*}$.

**Every edge of $P^*$ other than $e_{k^*}$ survives.** Take any edge $e_j$ of $P^*$ with $j < k^*$. By the characterization, $e_j$ is closed only if $e_j$ lies on a Hamiltonian path of $G_j$. But $G_j \subseteq G_{k^*-1}$, and $G_{k^*-1}$ has no Hamiltonian path (by minimality of $k^*$); a subgraph of a graph with no Hamiltonian path has none either. So $G_j$ has no Hamiltonian path, and $e_j \notin R$.

**Counting components.** The edge $e_{k^*}$ is closed (it lies on the Hamiltonian path $P^*$ of $G_{k^*}$ and is its heaviest edge). All other edges of $P^*$ survive. Removing the single edge $e_{k^*}$ from the path $P^*$ splits it into two paths whose vertex sets partition all the cities. Hence in $G \setminus R$ every city belongs to one of these two surviving paths, so

$$G \setminus R \text{ has at most two connected components}.$$

Therefore no three cities can lie in three pairwise distinct components. In particular it is impossible that $A$, $B$, and $C$ are simultaneously pairwise disconnected. This contradicts the reported outcome, so the king's order must have been followed incorrectly. $\blacksquare$

